              'Your body is made of trillions of tiny units called cells. A cell is the smallest unit of life that can carry out the jobs a living thing needs: taking in energy, making parts, and responding to its surroundings. Most cells are far too small to see without a microscope.\n\nA cell works a bit like a small factory. The cell membrane is a thin skin that decides what goes in and out. The nucleus holds DNA, the set of instructions for building the body\'s proteins. Mitochondria are the power stations: they release usable energy from the sugar and oxygen the cell takes in. Ribosomes read instructions and build proteins, and proteins do much of the cell\'s work.\n\nNot all cells look alike, because different cells have different jobs. A red blood cell is shaped like a flattened disc and carries oxygen. A nerve cell is long and thin so it can send signals across the body. A muscle cell contains fibers that can shorten, which makes movement possible. Even though they look different, almost all of your cells carry the same DNA. What differs is which instructions each cell uses.\n\nCells also divide to make new cells. This is how you grow, and how a scraped knee heals. Doctors care about cells because most diseases begin when cells are damaged, invaded by germs, or stop following their normal rules.',
