              'Health claims are everywhere: ads, videos, social posts, labels. Some are accurate, some are hopeful guesses, and some are made to sell something. A few habits help you sort them out.\n\nFirst, ask who is making the claim and what they gain. A company selling a supplement has a reason to say it works. That does not make it false, but it means you should look for independent evidence. Second, ask what the evidence is. A personal testimonial, such as \"this tea cured my cold,\" is only a story. Colds go away by themselves, so the tea gets the credit. Stronger evidence comes from well-designed trials with a control group, and strongest of all is a review that combines many good studies.\n\nThird, watch the wording. Words like miracle, secret, detox, cure-all, or \"doctors hate this\" are warning signs. Real medicine rarely fixes everything. Fourth, remember that correlation is not causation. If people who eat breakfast do better at school, it may be that breakfast helps, or that something else, such as a stable home life, explains both. Fifth, check the size of an effect. A \"50 percent higher risk\" of something very rare can still be a tiny risk.\n\nGood sources include national health agencies, university hospitals, and your own doctor or pharmacist. Before sharing a claim, pause and check it. If the claim is about something that could cause harm, such as stopping a prescribed medicine, ask a health professional first.',
