              'First aid is the help you give to an injured or ill person until professional help takes over. The first step is always safety. Check that the scene is safe for you before going near someone, because you cannot help if you become hurt too. Then call the local emergency number, or ask another person to call, and tell them where you are and what has happened.\n\nFor bleeding, press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth and keep pressing. For a minor burn, cool it under cool running water for several minutes, and do not use ice or butter. For a nosebleed, sit up, lean slightly forward and pinch the soft part of the nose. For a suspected broken bone, keep the person still and call for help. Choking in a person who cannot cough, speak or breathe needs immediate action from a trained person, often a series of back blows and abdominal thrusts.\n\nCPR, or cardiopulmonary resuscitation, is for someone who is unresponsive and not breathing normally. When the heart stops, blood stops carrying oxygen to the brain, and every minute without help lowers the chance of survival. The simplest CPR is hands-only: call for help, then push hard and fast in the center of the chest at about 100 to 120 pushes per minute, allowing the chest to rise between pushes. Keep going until help takes over. An AED, or automated external defibrillator, is a portable device that gives voice instructions and can shock a stopped heart back into a normal rhythm, and anyone can use it.\n\nA certified course from a group such as the Red Cross or the American Heart Association gives hands-on practice, and many courses are open to middle school students.',
